{"subjectId":"c","id":"strings","title":"Strings in C","group":"Arrays & Strings","order":20,"html":"<p class=\"lead\">C has no string type. A string is an array of <code>char</code> terminated by a <strong>NUL byte</strong> <code>'\\0'</code> — every string function walks the bytes until it finds that terminator.</p>\n<section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-arrows-diff\"></i>Arrays vs pointers to literals</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"c\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;\n#include &lt;string.h&gt;\n\nint main(void) {\n    char s1[] = \"hello\";            /* array of 6 bytes (a writable copy) */\n    char s2[10] = \"hi\";             /* rest zero-filled */\n    const char *s3 = \"world\";       /* pointer to a read-only literal */\n\n    s1[0] = 'H';                    /* fine: s1 is our own array */\n    printf(\"%s %s %s\\n\", s1, s2, s3);\n    printf(\"sizeof: %zu %zu %zu\\n\", sizeof s1, sizeof s2, sizeof s3);\n    printf(\"strlen: %zu %zu %zu\\n\", strlen(s1), strlen(s2), strlen(s3));\n    return 0;\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>Hello hi world\nsizeof: 6 10 8\nstrlen: 5 2 5</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th></th><th><code>char s[] = \"hello\";</code></th><th><code>const char *p = \"hello\";</code></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>What it is</td><td>Array of 6 <code>char</code>, initialised from the literal</td><td>Pointer to a literal stored in read-only memory</td></tr><tr><td>Modifiable?</td><td>Yes</td><td><strong>No</strong> — writing is undefined behaviour (usually a crash)</td></tr><tr><td><code>sizeof</code></td><td>6 (includes <code>\\0</code>)</td><td>8 (size of a pointer)</td></tr><tr><td>Reassignable?</td><td>No (<code>s = \"x\"</code> is an error)</td><td>Yes (<code>p = \"x\"</code> points elsewhere)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-circle-dot\"></i>The terminator is everything</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"c\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;\n\nint main(void) {\n    char word[] = {'c', 'a', 't', '\\0'};    /* same as \"cat\" */\n    printf(\"%s\\n\", word);\n    word[1] = '\\0';                         /* cut the string short */\n    printf(\"%s\\n\", word);\n    for (int i = 0; i &lt; 4; i++) printf(\"%d \", word[i]);\n    printf(\"\\n\");\n    return 0;\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>cat\nc\n99 0 116 0</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-code\"></i>Writing string functions yourself</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"c\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;\n#include &lt;ctype.h&gt;\n\nsize_t my_strlen(const char *s) {\n    const char *p = s;\n    while (*p) p++;\n    return (size_t)(p - s);\n}\n\nvoid reverse(char *s) {\n    size_t n = my_strlen(s);\n    for (size_t i = 0, j = n ? n - 1 : 0; i &lt; j; i++, j--) {\n        char t = s[i]; s[i] = s[j]; s[j] = t;\n    }\n}\n\nint is_palindrome(const char *s) {\n    size_t i = 0, j = my_strlen(s);\n    while (i &lt; j) {\n        if (tolower((unsigned char)s[i]) != tolower((unsigned char)s[j - 1])) return 0;\n        i++; j--;\n    }\n    return 1;\n}\n\nint count_words(const char *s) {\n    int words = 0, in_word = 0;\n    for (; *s; s++) {\n        if (isspace((unsigned char)*s)) in_word = 0;\n        else if (!in_word) { in_word = 1; words++; }\n    }\n    return words;\n}\n\nint main(void) {\n    char buf[] = \"stressed\";\n    reverse(buf);\n    printf(\"%s %zu\\n\", buf, my_strlen(buf));\n    printf(\"%d %d\\n\", is_palindrome(\"Racecar\"), is_palindrome(\"hello\"));\n    printf(\"%d\\n\", count_words(\"  the quick  brown fox \"));\n    return 0;\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>desserts 8\n1 0\n4</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-transform\"></i>Converting between strings and numbers</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"c\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;\n#include &lt;stdlib.h&gt;\n\nint main(void) {\n    char *end;\n    long n = strtol(\"  1234xyz\", &amp;end, 10);       /* stops at the first non-digit */\n    printf(\"%ld, rest = \\\"%s\\\"\\n\", n, end);\n    printf(\"%ld %ld\\n\", strtol(\"ff\", NULL, 16), strtol(\"101\", NULL, 2));\n    printf(\"%.2f\\n\", strtod(\"3.14159\", NULL));\n    printf(\"%d\\n\", atoi(\"42\"));                   /* no error reporting at all */\n\n    char out[32];\n    int len = snprintf(out, sizeof out, \"%d-%s-%.1f\", 7, \"ab\", 2.5);\n    printf(\"%s (%d chars)\\n\", out, len);\n    return 0;\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>1234, rest = \"xyz\"\n255 5\n3.14\n42\n7-ab-2.5 (8 chars)</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"callout callout--note\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Key point</div><p>Prefer <code>strtol</code> / <code>strtod</code> over <code>atoi</code> / <code>atof</code>: they report where parsing stopped and set <code>errno</code> to <code>ERANGE</code> on overflow. Use <code>snprintf</code> (never <code>sprintf</code>) to build strings safely.</p></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-alert-triangle\"></i>Pitfalls</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"c\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C — writing to a string literal (bus error / segfault at run time)</figcaption><pre><code>int main(void) {\n    char *s = \"hello\";      /* legal in C (not C++), but the literal is read-only */\n    s[0] = 'H';             /* undefined behaviour: crashes on most systems */\n    return 0;\n}</code></pre></figure>\n<ul><li>Forgetting room for <code>'\\0'</code>: <code>char s[5] = \"hello\";</code> compiles in C but has no terminator.</li><li>Comparing with <code>==</code> compares <em>addresses</em> — use <code>strcmp</code>.</li><li>Returning a pointer to a local <code>char</code> array from a function (it dies on return).</li><li>Passing a plain <code>char</code> (possibly negative) to <code>isalpha</code> / <code>toupper</code> — cast to <code>unsigned char</code> first.</li></ul>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>What is the difference between char s[] = \"abc\" and char *s = \"abc\"?</summary><div><p>The first creates a 4-byte array on the stack (or in <code>.data</code>) initialised with a copy of the literal — you can modify it. The second creates a pointer to the literal itself, which lives in read-only memory; modifying it is undefined behaviour. <code>sizeof</code> gives 4 vs 8.</p></div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>How is the length of a C string determined?</summary><div><p>By scanning for the NUL terminator — <code>strlen</code> is O(n). The length is not stored anywhere, which is why repeated <code>strlen</code> calls in a loop condition (<code>for (i = 0; i &lt; strlen(s); i++)</code>) make a loop O(n²).</p></div></details></section>"}